    type: "TTP",
    title: "Holding Seoul at Risk: Targeting Civilians",
    what: ["Analysis: North Korea aims long-range artillery at Seoul’s civilian population as a core deterrent. There is no open-source sign of a legal review or civilian harm process.", "Update: the 2025 study estimates about 2,600 civilian deaths in Seoul in a crisis attack, about 4,600 in a surprise attack, and 700 to 1,100 if the US and ROK struck first. It cites RAND (2020) at 87,600 to 130,000 casualties, 6,620 to 10,680 of them fatal, for an hour-long barrage."],
    how: ["170mm guns and 240mm rockets near the DMZ hold greater Seoul at risk; state media said the September 2026 rocket test was meant to “instill fear in South Koreans”.", "At Yeonpyeong in 2010, KPA fire killed two civilians and two marines, injured three civilians and 17 soldiers, and set more than 50 homes on fire.", "North Korean missiles sent to Russia in 2024 were fired at populated areas and civilian infrastructure in Ukraine, including Kyiv and Zaporizhzhia.", "Reported: casualty models for a barrage on Seoul range from up to 250,000 casualties (older US estimates) to about 2,600 civilian deaths in a crisis (2025 study), largely because of shelters and fast counter-battery fire."],
    why: ["Analysis: threatening civilians offsets conventional weakness by raising the political cost of action against the regime.", "Compare US targeting with legal review and collateral damage estimation, and Australia’s human approval of every strike."],
    sources: [{"title": "North Korea Military Power: A Growing Regional and Global Threat", "publisher": "US Defense Intelligence Agency", "year": "2021", "url": "https://www.dia.mil/Portals/110/Documents/News/North_Korea_Military_Power.pdf"}, {"title": "Lost Seoul: Assessing Pyongyang’s Other Deterrent", "publisher": "Texas National Security Review", "year": "2025", "url": "https://tnsr.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/TNSR-Vol-8-Issue-3_Anderson-Press.pdf"}, {"title": "Unlawful Military Cooperation including Arms Transfers between North Korea and Russia (MSMT/2025/1)", "publisher": "Multilateral Sanctions Monitoring Team", "year": "2025", "url": "https://msmt.info/view/save/2025/05/29/1085cade-a4b1-4405-94c0-7c980c24fd21-Unlawful_Military_Cooperation_including_Arms_Transfers_between_North_Korea_and_Russia_(MSMT_2025_1).pdf"}, {"title": "North Korea Says 240mm Rocket’s Range Upgraded to Instill Fear in South Koreans", "publisher": "NK News", "year": "2026", "url": "https://www.nknews.org/2026/09/north-korea-says-240mm-rockets-range-upgraded-to-instill-fear-in-south-koreans/"}, {"title": "Shelling of Yeonpyeong Island (International Relations of the Asia-Pacific)", "publisher": "Oxford Academic", "year": "", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/irap/article/18/2/193/2562777"}, {"title": "North Korea’s Conventional Artillery Threat (PE-262)", "publisher": "RAND Corporation", "year": "", "url": "https://www.rand.org/content/dam/rand/pubs/perspectives/PE200/PE262/RAND_PE262.pdf"}, {"title": "Mind the Gap Between Rhetoric and Reality", "publisher": "Nautilus Institute", "year": "2012", "url": "https://nautilus.org/napsnet/napsnet-special-reports/mind-the-gap-between-rhetoric-and-reality/"}, {"title": "Lost Seoul? Assessing Pyongyang’s Other Deterrent", "publisher": "Texas National Security Review", "year": "2025", "url": "https://tnsr.org/2025/06/lost-seoul-assessing-pyongyangs-other-deterrent/"}, {"title": "North Korean Conventional Artillery: A Means to Retaliate, Coerce, Deter, or Terrorize Populations", "publisher": "RAND Corporation", "year": "2020", "url": "https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA619-1.html"}],
    confidence: "Analysis",
